WEBVTT

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.700
TMNT 2, the arcade game.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:08.620
You may have Shredder double teamed, but he still has you outnumbered.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.580
TMNT 2, the arcade game.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:32.380
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:33.380
I'm Mike.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.880
I'm Sean.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:36.160
And I'm Joe.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.860
Is it possible to be outnumbered while you're being double teamed?

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:48.180
Yeah, I mean, that was a little bit of a weird, because being double teamed implies that there's two to one against you.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:50.780
Right, but I guess he has the whole entire Foot Clan.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.000
Right. What are we talking about?

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:55.460
The back of the box?

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.360
Double teamed? The turtle? Oh yeah, the feet.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:04.380
Oh. So here's what they call, they're called the Foot Clan because they have more toes than the Ninja Turtles.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.720
So they're bragging.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:07.860
They're like, look at us.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.180
We have real feet.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.340
True feet. We have feet.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.960
Yeah. I like that.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.980
Turtles have three fingers and three toes.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.080
Is that the end of the episode?

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.060
What happened? I was just going to say that all about do it.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:22.260
No comment? Yeah.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:23.720
Geez. No comment.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:24.960
Rough crowd. It's fine.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.280
We're all stunned by that fact of turtles.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:33.100
They're saying double teamed on the back of the box because you can play this two player at the same time.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.860
And, you know, take that is the preferred way to play any beat them up.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:48.480
It's like if there were two player beat them up, if it was four player beat them up, if it's six player beat them up, it's always best to play with the maximum number of players on screen because that's the most chaos.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.880
That's the most button mashing satisfaction.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:57.920
That's the most like way to stay alive in the game because you don't have to deal with all six at the same time.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.240
You can have your friends help you out.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:09.100
And that was clearly the intention with the game that this is based on, which is, of course, the Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles arcade game here on the NES.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:17.400
It is not a sequel to the first NES game, which we, you know, I don't want to say we were like harsh on it or whatever.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.940
We just weren't fans of it.

00:02:19.220 --> 00:02:24.200
It certainly gets a worse rap maybe than we gave it.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.360
So I think like in general, TMNT is a franchise we like and we want to like these games.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:41.360
And this one took the arcade game and brought it right to the NES with more levels and more boss fights and new features.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:50.140
So like who's to say that the old adage of the arcade game always being better than the 8-bit counterpart is true here.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.640
We're going to give this a real shake.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:54.160
I didn't know that there were more levels.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:59.400
I've never been very good at this game in the arcade.

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:07.660
I basically, you know, you put a couple quarters in you and whoever you're playing with die pretty quickly.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:10.920
And then you move on to another game, at least for me.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:21.540
I know that probably you guys and our target audience would have spent more time at the arcade machine than me.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.680
So I just had no idea that this was, this had more stuff.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.960
Well, I'm probably coming in.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:31.280
I also had no idea, but maybe for the opposite reason.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:35.560
I'm sure I played this in the arcade because it's kind of just ubiquitous.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.260
But like I feel like I don't really remember it so much in the arcade, but I did play this as a kid.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:51.140
I think the first TMNT and then the one that I actually liked, which I thought was maybe this one that we still didn't get to was it was the one where the second level is the surfing level.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.720
I used to love that. It's not this one.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.260
But me and my cousin used to play a lot of TMNT games.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:56.840
It's not Battletoads.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:58.320
No, it's TMNT.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.500
But this is one of the TMNT games I did play as a child.

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.960
And I also never knew that this was like an expansion on the original game.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:08.080
I just thought this was a port.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:12.320
It's worth mentioning and we'll get into it, of course, about the expansion part.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:20.880
I guess I'm just saying that like it has more than the original game, but it also could arguably have less right like the the presentation here.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:29.220
If you've ever looked at the arcade game or played it, it feels like it's, you know, a direct adaptation of the very, very popular cartoon.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:41.480
It's it's using that same art style and it's the cut scenes and the pixel art are doing that much better than the NES could hope to.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:52.240
So I think for me, this is the one that like I like grab my eyes a lot at the arcade was like, whoa, this is basically the cartoon now as a video game.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:05:11.240
And regardless of how you feel about beat them ups, it's the same reason why like the Simpsons arcade game and the X-Men arcade game are like fondly remembered, not just because they're good beat them ups, but because it's like it's taking the art style of that thing that you like.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:23.240
And the world of that thing that you like and making it into a fun little mindless video game where like the the characters that you've come to expect all make appearances.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:30.540
And you're just kind of like, OK, I died time to put in another quarter to keep experiencing what's happening.

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.000
And that's no different here.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:40.960
And whether you're playing as Donnie, Mikey, Raff or Leo, you know, it's fun to just like change it up with the weapon.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:47.440
But I've never thought about like, oh, well, I want to use the bow staff or I want to use the nunchucks.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:50.040
It's like, no, I just want to play as my favorite Ninja Turtle.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:54.020
And hopefully nobody else in my party, you know, picks the same.

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:57.460
Yeah, you make a good point that I hadn't really thought of until right now.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:05.400
But the fact that it's pretty rare for an NES game to have a pixel art that is so one to one to its source.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:14.780
Like even when something's based on, you know, a cartoon, I feel like a lot of times it's like this tiny little pixel that like you can like squint and imagine it's the same thing.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:25.620
Like not even just on like an artistic level, on a technical level, like it's kind of impressive that this does that these do look just like the cartoon characters, even in this like pixel form.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.680
I'm surprised I never noticed that we don't see that quite as much.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.900
Yeah. And when they try, it ends up looking kind of awful.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:45.040
Yeah. I mean, maybe TMNT is just like, especially that 80s TMNT is just like the perfect level of like not realistic looking enough, cartoony enough that you can kind of make it out of a few pixels as well.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.540
It's also a pretty realistic Pizza Hut logo.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:51.580
One to one. Yeah, yeah, for sure.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.740
And I don't know about you, but I'm craving some Pizza Hut right about now.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:55.940
Get a slice.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.700
Right. So go to PizzaHut.com and order your slice now.

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:00.860
Use the promo code.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:03.500
No, no, we're not chilling like the game did.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:11.500
And it's worth mentioning game was made by Konami, but they use the Ultra Games label for the NES.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:15.160
And that was I think we might have talked about this once before, so I'll be brief about it.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:22.920
But that's the workaround for Nintendo of America's licensing rules where they had like a certain number of games you could release.

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:31.340
And since Konami had hit that threshold, they were like, all right, we'll just open up another company and release the games under that.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:45.720
So Konami used Ultra Games as its like additional kind of funny, you know, that they would do that for something as like, I mean, TMNT is arguably at its hottest in 1990.

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:52.900
The movie, the live action movie where they wear the suits, which is still amazing and absolutely holds up.

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:55.900
That came out in March of 1990.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:08:00.380
So that's out and a huge success and was a big box office success.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.380
The cartoon is on season four.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.100
So it's having a run.

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:12.600
Merchandise is like all that stuff that you should have bought in 1990 and kept it in the box is now worth thousands of dollars.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:19.120
Because, you know, of course, you should have never played with your Technodrome and you should have just kept it in the box.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:21.900
Just like Mighty Morphin Power Rangers, too.

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:25.080
It's like, who was the freak who kept that shit in the box?

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:28.040
Like, guys, that stuff was meant to be played with, like, for real, real.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:32.100
I don't know if you asked me, I would have thought it would have been you, Mike.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:33.540
I feel like you have more boxes.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:35.520
Yeah, that does sound like a Mike thing.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:38.840
You have more boxes of at least old video games than anyone I know.

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:41.340
That's true, but that was my mom.

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:44.700
See, like the kids, I would have thrown out all those things in a heartbeat.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.460
My mom was the one who was, like, smart enough to realize we should keep them.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.560
But I guess what I'm talking about is, like, you know, there were some really nice TMNT sets.

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:56.360
Like, you know, the action figures themselves are worth good money, too.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:05.960
But there were some real, like, play sets or larger vehicles that, you know, now, especially in the boxes, are just, like, ridiculous numbers.

00:09:06.540 --> 00:09:12.340
Same with Mighty Morphin Power Rangers with the Zords, if you have those giant Zords still in the boxes.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.580
But it's like, those things were meant to be, like, smashed into each other.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:23.420
You were supposed to take one figure and smash it into the other figure, not preserve them for your investment portfolio.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.120
You got to buy two copies.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.560
And the game starts off with Shredder is back and he's kidnapped April.

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:32.040
What's new?

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:41.560
The Turtles have to – it's set up, like, scenes, which I guess further, you know, reinforces the whole cartoon thing.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:45.680
But each scene is just a different setting and a different boss.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:59.140
And so you basically otherwise are just doing the same thing that you do in any particular beat-em-up where you walk for a little bit, encounter a bunch of enemies, take down all enemies, and suddenly you're allowed to start scrolling again.

00:10:00.580 --> 00:10:01.800
Do we want to talk?

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:11.300
Can you guys remind me, like, what April does for the – like, is she, like, their collective girlfriend?

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:13.100
Is it, like, a big holly?

00:10:13.100 --> 00:10:16.480
Well, she's, like, a reporter in this iteration, right?

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:21.240
And then I think one of them is, like, the love interest.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.420
Well, yeah, but one of them is, like, crushing on her the whole time.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.000
And it's different in the Nickelodeon version than the –

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:33.160
Okay. So, like, yeah, let's remove the romantic entanglement.

00:10:34.020 --> 00:10:36.920
Like, let's just say that this is, like, a workplace.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:40.080
Like, what does she do for the team?

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:41.860
Is she just there to be –

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:42.960
She's got the scoop.

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:47.200
She can tell them where, you know, where, what, and things are happening in the city.

00:10:48.020 --> 00:10:49.580
Yeah. Also, she's, like, a journalist.

00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:54.720
Yeah, yeah. Yeah, she's, like, the lowest lane to Clark Kent, you know, is my –

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.660
She's a collector, Clark Kent.

00:10:57.380 --> 00:11:05.780
I don't know as much about the 80s cartoon, but that was always my understanding of it.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:16.200
And, you know, as far as, like, any romance between April and the Turtles, like, it is possible, Sean, to just also, like, want to save your friend.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:22.680
Oh, I get it. Like, if you were kidnapped, I would hope that I would be able to rescue you.

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:25.180
Probably not successfully, but I would try to.

00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:33.640
And I would hope that the news wouldn't cover it as, you know, host of Nostalgia Michael Esposito tries to rescue his lover, Sean.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:43.360
I've never – yeah, I've never been in a situation where I was trying to rescue someone that I wasn't in a romantic relationship with.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:45.320
Right. Well, I mean, it's true, though.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:46.600
I get it.

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:53.660
Like, video games have trained us that you're rescuing a princess with the expectation that you're going to get a fucking kiss at the end.

00:11:55.140 --> 00:12:01.900
See, but it's funny because, like, is it ever implied in, like, most of these games that, like, you're going to get a kiss?

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:03.520
Like, does Mario get a kiss?

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:06.960
He does. Okay, does Link?

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:11.620
That turns out that, like, Princess Peach is actually, like, his aunt.

00:12:12.800 --> 00:12:15.720
Right. Sister, actually.

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.940
Same thing. Yeah.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:19.640
Sorry, we're getting off topic.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:20.720
You're thinking of Star Wars.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:23.620
It's okay to get off topic.

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:35.760
But if you want me to get us back on topic, I guess this is, you know, since I mentioned that you're not necessarily playing this game with a strategy in mind of, like, I've got to use the bow staff, right?

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.240
You're playing with your favorite turtle.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:41.820
Who'd you pick? I picked Leo because that was the first option.

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:47.160
I don't really have any, like, you are the turtle guy.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:48.580
I've never really cared about this game.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:50.400
I think that's why, because you managed to pick incorrectly.

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:52.280
Okay. Hold on.

00:12:52.340 --> 00:12:54.760
Like, there was no wrong answer, and then you chose Leo.

00:12:55.500 --> 00:12:59.880
Because I was going to say, I think it is maybe a boring answer, but I also picked Leo.

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:01.100
I just like swords.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.080
And I like blue.

00:13:02.520 --> 00:13:04.600
So I always used to pick Leo.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:08.260
Yeah. I don't have any attachment to these guys.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:16.840
I always did, like, see, and this is maybe where we'll get into, like, the gameplay and the differences.

00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:28.020
I always liked, in some of these TMNT games, trying out with the swords and trying, and then, like, trying the bow staff and, like, seeing, oh, what different moves do they have based on, you know, based on their weapon.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:31.240
And there's not that many moves in this game.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:33.020
Yeah. There's a tack.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:35.080
So it, like, doesn't feel like it matters too much.

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:39.860
I will say, I'm saying this right now, admittedly, in this playthrough, I just played as Leo the whole time.

00:13:40.020 --> 00:13:44.920
I guess I'm making some assumptions here about playing as Donnie or Mikey or whoever.

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:48.800
But it just doesn't feel like there's too much difference.

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:03.080
So Sean was going to cover it, and he got 50% of the way there.

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:05.860
There's a tack, and there's jumping attack.

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:08.760
Yeah. Right.

00:14:09.200 --> 00:14:20.140
So that is the extent of the game, and that is the extent of the Ninja Turtles' personalities, at least in terms of inputs that you can do.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:26.880
So the only changeup between them is that it isn't just a pixel art weapon.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:32.680
The weapon actually does impact their reach and how fast they can attack.

00:14:32.680 --> 00:14:42.500
So as you can imagine, it is a pretty simple formula of Donatello with the staff has the longest reach, but it is the slowest attack.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:50.260
And Raph has the shortest reach with the, I think I'm saying this right, the size?

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:51.920
Forks? Yeah.

00:14:52.220 --> 00:14:54.000
I think I'm saying this right, dinner forks.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:00.100
And it is the fastest attack, though, in the game.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.600
So if you miss, it's kind of like whatever.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.000
You can go back to attacking.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:13.000
Whereas if you miss with Donnie, you know, especially in the later stages, some of those more pesky enemies will start to be able to barrage you.

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.160
But that's like it.

00:15:14.320 --> 00:15:15.640
And so that's kind of strange, right?

00:15:15.780 --> 00:15:20.760
Like that this is the whole personification of the Turtles' personality.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:27.200
Arguably the most interesting part about the show is the four Turtles, and this is how they get covered.

00:15:27.200 --> 00:15:36.620
And meanwhile, like all the enemies have various attack pools, and all the bosses have, you know, like flavors specific to them.

00:15:37.660 --> 00:15:44.540
You know, like I'm thinking about Rocksteady and his charge at you, and Baxter.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:48.400
And you fight Baxter twice in this one, or is that the arcade?

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.180
I think you fight him twice in this one.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.640
Once in the machine, and then once as the fly.

00:15:54.340 --> 00:15:56.280
And, you know, so like they're playing into those.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:57.580
Yeah, they're playing into those.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:06.340
The fly is pretty later in the game, but the machine one where he's just like teleporting above the ground, and he has those annoying.

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:10.480
I wish I knew the name of those guys, but they're like little pet dino things.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:12.520
Yeah. Those things.

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:13.780
Oh, I hate those. Yeah, those are annoying.

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:15.920
That's what I'm saying, though. It's like, okay, cool.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:22.980
That's like, that's some interesting stuff to both fight and take down, while you feel pretty generic.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:30.860
Yeah, there's two things missing for me that I feel like if one of them were there, I'd be a little more okay with it.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:36.280
One is that there's no difference, really, other than the minor one you mentioned, between the turtles.

00:16:36.300 --> 00:16:38.460
So your character select doesn't really matter that much.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:41.540
And the other is that you don't, there's no evolution.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:45.720
There's no pickups of different weapons, or you don't learn another move at any point during this game.

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:58.060
And like, I don't need that in every game, but like not having either of those things in this game being, you know, kind of long for a beat-em-up, is, to me, is a little hard to forgive.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:00.340
Because it does get just so samey.

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:01.860
There's only one thing you can do.

00:17:01.920 --> 00:17:04.080
I mean, yes, you can jump kick, and you can swing.

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:05.800
But like, I don't know.

00:17:05.880 --> 00:17:10.080
Because like in the arcade game, there was another button, right?

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:12.980
Like, wasn't there like attack and kick or something?

00:17:13.060 --> 00:17:14.640
Or was it all just one attack?

00:17:14.640 --> 00:17:16.260
I only remember one attack, too.

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.900
I will look that up for us, but I only remember one.

00:17:21.280 --> 00:17:31.420
And I think I'm like, came into this conflating it with other NES PM&T games, where there's like, everyone has like, just one little super attack.

00:17:31.420 --> 00:17:35.820
Where like someone did like a, I think Leo did like a spin with both his swords out at his side.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:37.320
And like, yeah.

